No service worker, so this is plain browser HTTP caching. Two different things are happening:

The favicon image itself is cached normally. It's a plain `<img src>`, so once `https://acme.com/favicon.ico` loads, every other icon pointing at the same endpoint reuses the browser's copy, across renders and across page reloads, for as long as the origin's cache headers allow.

The probing is not cached. Which of the (up to six) candidate URLs actually works is tracked in `useState` inside each `Favicon` instance, so it's discarded on unmount and never shared between instances:

```12:25:apps/frontend/src/components/ui/favicon.tsx
export function Favicon({ url, fallback, className }: FaviconProps) {
	const [failed, setFailed] = useState<string[]>([]);

	const candidates = useMemo(() => getFaviconCandidates(url ?? undefined), [url]);
	const src = candidates.find((candidate) => !failed.includes(candidate));
```

That matters most in the worst case: an endpoint with no favicon at all. Radix unmounts the select content when the dropdown closes, so every time you open the model picker each icon walks the candidate list again from the top, and misses are only cheap if the server sends cache headers on its 404s — many don't. This is inherited behaviour, the MCP icon has always worked this way.

A module-level `Map<url, resolvedSrc | null>` would fix it: probe once per page session, share the result across every icon, and remember the "nothing worked" verdict so repeat opens go straight to the fallback glyph.

Without a service worker, the browser's standard HTTP cache handles favicon reuse across the page and reloads as long as cache headers are present. The tricky part is that each Favicon component instance tracks its own resolution state in React, which gets discarded when the component unmounts—so reopening the model picker will re-run the resolution logic since Radix Select unmounts its content when closed.

For providers without a favicon, that means up to 6 requests per icon each time the picker opens, and 404s often aren't cached depending on server headers. This matches the existing behavior for MCP icons. I should offer a module-level cache to avoid repeating this work.

