The chat input no longer touches the base URL at all, and the lookup elsewhere is now persisted.

Two changes:

`chat-input-model-select.tsx` is back to its original state — no `baseUrl` passed, so a custom endpoint shows the neutral server glyph in the chat. That component renders on every chat load and its dropdown remounts on every open, which made it the wrong place for a network-dependent icon. Favicons now appear only on pages an admin opens deliberately: the provider cards in settings, the automation and recommendation model pickers, and the Slack/Teams/Telegram/WhatsApp sections.

The probe result is now remembered in `localStorage` for a week, keyed by endpoint origin, so those admin pages also stop re-probing:

```17:23:apps/frontend/src/lib/favicon.ts
export const resolveFaviconCandidates = (url?: string | null): string[] => {
	const known = getKnownFavicon(url);
	if (known) {
		return known.src ? [known.src] : [];
	}
	return getFaviconCandidates(url ?? undefined);
};
```

`Favicon` records the winning URL on load and records "no icon" when the last candidate fails. The second case is the valuable one: an endpoint that serves no favicon is asked once, then produces zero requests until the entry goes stale, instead of walking all six candidates on every mount. A verdict is shared by every endpoint on the same origin, so several named providers behind one host probe once between them. Writes are wrapped in a try/catch, so a browser with storage disabled just keeps the verdict in memory for that page load.

I kept `baseUrl` on `getProjectAvailableModels` since the automations form, recommendations page, and messaging sections still use it.

Lint and type checks pass; `favicon.test.ts` now covers the cache with 11 tests, including that a stored verdict survives a page reload and that it expires after a week.

If you'd rather have zero favicon traffic from non-admins altogether, the messaging config sections are the remaining spot a regular member could reach — say the word and I'll gate those too.

